<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Colombia–Peru War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1932">1932</a>–<a href="/years/1933">1933</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Leticia Incident · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> War was fought between September 1932 and May 1933 after Peruvian civilians and soldiers seized the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> river port of Leticia, which Peru had ceded to Colombia under the Salomón–Lozano Treaty. Fighting along the Putumayo and Amazon rivers ended after the assassination of Peru's president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-sanchez-cerro">Luis Sánchez Cerro</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> mediation returned Leticia to Colombia, confirmed by the Rio de Janeiro Protocol of 1934.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/colombia-peru-war.jpg" alt="Colombia–Peru War" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Army of Colombia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guerra_peru1_1932_d.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The boundary treaty of 24 March 1922 had given <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> the Leticia district and access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a>, but most of its people were Peruvian, and heavy tariffs on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> added to the grievance. On 27 August 1932 Peruvian civilians led by Oscar Ordoñez and Juan La Rosa Guevara founded the Patriotic Junta of Loreto, whose Leticia Plan denounced the treaty and called for a peaceful recovery of the port by civilians alone. A force of 48 gathered at the border town of Caballococha, and after the seizure on 1 September Colombian officials and police fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>'s government took notice only on 17 September, when Peruvian forces on the Putumayo began stopping trade ships bound for Leticia. Laureano Gómez, head of the Senate minority, called for war on the border, El Tiempo reported more than 10,000 letters demanding it, and the Senate voted 10 million dollars for a fleet under General Alfredo Vásquez Cobo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-sanchez-cerro">Luis Miguel Sánchez Cerro</a> believed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>, without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> roads or a proper navy, could not respond. Yet by December 1932 Vásquez Cobo had reached the Amazon with old gunboats and new British destroyers, and German aviators of SCADTA, the airline that later became Avianca, fitted their planes as a temporary air force. President Enrique Olaya Herrera at first held back, hoping for a diplomatic settlement, and commanders quarrelled before Olaya named Vásquez Cobo Minister of War and pressed an attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarapaca">Tarapacá</a>.</p><p>The Colombians struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarapaca">Tarapacá</a> first, as a softer target than Leticia. On 13 March 1933 a landing at Puerto Hilario was driven off by Peruvian troops and aircraft, ending an attempt to threaten Leticia from behind, but Colombian troops occupied Gueppi on 26 March and in May took the Peruvian seaplane base on the Algodón River. On 26 May ten Colombian soldiers, unaware that a suspension of hostilities had been agreed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> the day before, captured four Peruvian officers and 77 soldiers at La Zoila.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>'s forces in Leticia were never driven out. Sánchez Cerro was shot dead on 30 April 1933, and 15 days later his successor, Óscar Benavides, met Alfonso López Pumarejo, head of the Colombian Liberal Party, to agree that Leticia be handed to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> commission. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-protocol">Rio de Janeiro Protocol</a> of 1934, which reaffirmed the Salomón–Lozano Treaty, Peru declared that it sincerely deplored the events that began in September 1932.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Colombia–Peru War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the twentieth century Peru fought Colombia over <b>Leticia</b> in 1932–1933 and Ecuador in 1941, 1981 and 1995, and from 1980 to 2000 the armed forces waged a brutal counter-insurgency against the Shining Path.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-peru">Republic of Peru · Military</a></li></ul></section>
